.Dd November 12, 2026
.Dt TIMER_CREATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timer_create ,
.Nm timer_settime ,
.Nm timer_gettime ,
.Nm timer_getoverrun ,
.Nm timer_delete
.Nd POSIX timers of a process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In signal.h
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn timer_create "clockid_t clockid" "struct sigevent *sev" "timer_t *timerid"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_settime "timer_t timerid" "int flags" "const struct itimerspec *new" "struct itimerspec *old"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_gettime "timer_t timerid" "struct itimerspec *cur"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_getoverrun "timer_t timerid"
.Ft int
.Fn timer_delete "timer_t timerid"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn timer_create ,
number 259 on i386 and 222 on x86-64, makes a timer on the clock
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
or a clock of processor time:
.Dv CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID ,
.Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID ,
or the clock of a process that
.Xr clock_getcpuclockid 3
gives or of a thread of the caller's that
.Xr pthread_getcpuclockid 3
gives.
The timer is made disarmed, and its id, the lowest the process does not
have, is stored at
.Fa timerid .
.Fa sev
says how it is notified:
.Dv SIGEV_SIGNAL
posts the signal
.Fa sigev_signo
to the process,
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
to the thread
.Fa sigev_notify_thread_id
of the process, and
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
nothing; a NULL
.Fa sev
is
.Dv SIGALRM
with the id as its value.
The C libraries make
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
of
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
and a thread of their own.
A process has at most 64 timers, a deleted timer whose signal still waits
to be posted counting among them until it is posted or discarded, as Linux
counts the signal such a timer leaves queued against the process's limit
of pending signals.
.Pp
.Fn timer_settime ,
number 260 on i386 and 223 on x86-64, arms and disarms the timer as
.Xr timerfd_settime 2
arms a timerfd, with
.Dv TIMER_ABSTIME
for an absolute time, and
.Fn timer_gettime ,
number 261 on i386 and 224 on x86-64, reports its setting as
.Xr timerfd_gettime 2
does.
The i386 64-bit time forms are numbers 409 and 408; x86-64 has none, since
its times are 64 bits already.
Times and intervals are kept and expire as
.Xr timerfd_settime 2
keeps them: to the nanosecond, when the clock reaches the time, never
early.
A timer expires between ticks when it is set and while its last
expiration set its signal pending or that signal has been taken since;
an expiration while its signal is pending leaves the timer to the 1 ms
ticks until the signal is taken, as Linux rearms such a timer only at the
signal's delivery, so that a short interval cannot raise interrupt after
interrupt.
When the signal is taken, the intervals of the setting in force that ended
since the signal was posted, past the one it stands for, are added to its
overrun and the timer's time moves past the clock, as Linux forwards the
timer then.
Only a signal posted for the setting in force does so: one left pending
across a
.Fn timer_settime ,
or posted by a deleted timer whose id a new timer took, is delivered with
the overrun it has and leaves the timer as it is, as Linux compares the
signal's mark with the timer's setting.
While a timer's own signal of an earlier setting is pending, the first
expiration of the setting in force adds one to that signal's overrun and
makes it the new setting's, as Linux's send_sigqueue adds to the overrun of
a timer's signal still queued; nothing adds to a signal a deleted timer of
the same id left pending, nor to a user's
.Dv SI_TIMER
signal.
The signal's overrun is counted before interrupts come on again, so no
expiration in between posts the signal a second time.
An expiration whose signal the process ignores leaves a timer of an
interval under a tick to the ticks, and keeps one of a tick or more
expiring between ticks, as Linux restarts such a timer at once and moves it
a tick on only when its interval is shorter.
An expiration that waits for another sender's pending signal of the same
number leaves the timer to the ticks until that signal is taken, which
posts the waiting timer's signal at once, the oldest first when several
wait, as Linux queues a signal for each timer and delivers them in turn.
A
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
timer expires at ticks alone.
.Fn timer_gettime
first counts the intervals the clock has passed of a timer left to the
ticks, so its time left is never reported as passed: into the overrun
the pending signal's delivery adds, or for
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
into nothing, as Linux's does; an expired one-shot
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
timer has no time left, and any other timer whose time has passed reports
a nanosecond left, its signal not yet delivered.
The time left is counted from the reading of the clock the timer was moved
on by, as Linux's common_timer_get counts both from one reading.
.Pp
The timer tick, which runs without the big lock, finds an expiration and
leaves its signal for the next holder of the lock: the interrupt between
ticks, which takes the lock and posts the signal at once, or a later tick
that takes the lock, which may come some ticks after the expiration.
The expirations counted until then join that one signal, whose delivery
counts them with the other intervals since.
A
.Fn timer_settime
or a
.Fn timer_delete
posts such a signal first, as Linux sent it at expiration; an
.Xr execve 2
drops it with the timers' other signals.
.Pp
An expiration posts the signal with
.Va si_code
.Dv SI_TIMER ,
the timer's id in
.Va si_timerid ,
the value
.Fa sigev_value
in
.Va si_value ,
and in
.Va si_overrun
every interval that ended after the first expiration and before the signal
was taken under the setting in force, whether or not a signal was
pending, as Linux counts them when it rearms the timer at the signal's
delivery: an absolute timer of a 1 s interval armed for a time 10 s past
delivers its first signal with an overrun of 10, and expirations while the
signal is pending are counted too.
While a signal of the same number that is not the timer's is pending, the
timer's signal waits, and is posted when that signal is taken; a one-shot
timer's is posted then too.
A waiting signal outlives a
.Fn timer_settime ,
whose first expiration adds one to its overrun, and a
.Fn timer_delete ,
as Linux leaves the timer's signal queued; a signal of a former setting,
or of a deleted timer, is delivered with only that overrun and moves no
timer, so the intervals of a setting that a
.Fn timer_settime
or a
.Fn timer_delete
ended are not counted, as on Linux.
Installing
.Dv SIG_IGN ,
or a default that ignores, for a signal discards the timers' signals of
that number, waiting ones and a deleted timer's among them, and so does a
stop signal for
.Dv SIGCONT
and
.Dv SIGCONT
for the stop signals.
.Fn timer_getoverrun ,
number 262 on i386 and 225 on x86-64, gives the overrun that the setting
in force counted for the last signal of a periodic timer delivered since
the timer was set, as Linux's it_overrun_last: the intervals of that
setting that ended after the first of its expirations the signal stands
for; 0 before the first delivery and for a one-shot timer, and unchanged
by expirations after that delivery.
It leaves out what the signal's
.Va si_overrun
carried of an earlier setting: a signal left pending across a
.Fn timer_settime
and taken after the new setting's first expiration, before its next, has
an overrun of 1, and
.Fn timer_getoverrun
gives 0, as on Linux.
.Pp
.Fn timer_delete ,
number 263 on i386 and 226 on x86-64, disarms and deletes the timer.
.Xr execve 2
and the end of the process delete its timers, and
.Xr execve 2
discards their signals, pending or waiting, keeping other senders', as
Linux's exec does; a child of
.Xr fork 2
has none.
.Pp
A timer on a clock of processor time counts the ticks the timer interrupt
charges to the process's threads, or to the one thread, as
.Xr times 2
counts them, user ticks alone for a clock of user time; its time left and
its interval are in ticks, and it expires at the tick that uses up the
last of its time and is armed again at that tick; the expirations of the
ticks before its signal is posted go into that signal's overrun.
A timer on another process's clock keeps counting that process until it
is deleted, and counts nothing once the process has ended.
.Pp
On x86-64 the timer calls take itimerspecs of 64-bit fields, and there
are no time64 forms.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn timer_getoverrun
returns the overrun, and the others 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
musl's
.Fn timer_delete
returns the negative error number itself.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EAGAIN
The process has 64 timers, deleted ones whose signals still wait among
them, or the kernel had no memory for another.
.It Er EFAULT
A structure is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
The clock, the notification or its signal is not one of those above, a
thread
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD_ID
names is not one of the process's,
.Fa timerid
is not a timer of the process, a flag is not
.Dv TIMER_ABSTIME ,
.Fa new
is NULL, or a time is negative or its nanoseconds are outside 0 to
999999999.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr setitimer 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr timerfd 2
